fact: Artifact, unit: ArtifactUnit) -> str:\n    if unit.inline_text is not None:\n        return unit.inline_text\n    lines = artifact.text.splitlines()\n    return \"\\n\".join(lines[unit.start_line - 1 : max(unit.start_line - 1, unit.end_line - 1)])\n```\n\nThe issue here: `end_line` is inclusive. So if `start_line=1` and `end_line=5`, we need lines 1 through 5 (inclusive). The slice should be `lines[start_line - 1 : end_line]` (not `end_line - 1`). \n\nWait, let me re-read: `lines[unit.start_line - 1 : max(unit.start_line - 1, unit.end_line - 1)]`\n\nIf start_line=1 and end_line=5:\n- `unit.start_line - 1` = 0\n- `max(0, 4)` = 4\n- `lines[0:4]` gives lines at indices 0,1,2,3 which are lines 1,2,3,4 — missing line 5!\n\nThe correct slice should be `lines[unit.start_line - 1 : unit.end_line]` to get lines 1 through 5 (indices 0 through 4).\n\nSo the fix is: change `max(unit.start_line - 1, unit.end_line - 1)` to `unit.end_line`.\n\n**In `reopen.py`, the truncation logic:**\n```python\ntruncated = len(exact_text) >= max_chars\n```\n\nThe contract says: \"ReopenReceipt.truncated is true only when the exact text is strictly longer than max_chars; equality is complete.\"\n\nSo it should be `len(exact_text) > max_chars` (strictly greater than, not >=).\n\nAnd then:\n```python\nmaterial"}
